since russia's invasion of ukraine on  february 24th countries around the world  have responded with sweeping sanctions  nato countries fearing that any kind of  military engagement in the conflict  could trigger a fully fledged war with  the rest of europe and its allies have  opted for an economic offensive instead  countries around the world have frozen  russian assets they've barred the  majority of russian banks from the swift  international payment system and they've  sanctioned russian oligarchs and more  new restrictions intended to [ __ ] the  russian economy are being announced on a  near daily basis and the rupal's value  has plummeted with economic forecasters  predicting the russian economy will  shrink by 7 to 15 this year  but will the aggressive sanctions be  enough to stop russia's war in ukraine  and what impact are they likely to have  in russia and across the world joining  me to discuss this is sergey goryev  economist and scientific director of the  master and phd programs in economics as  seance po paris thank you so much for  joining me on upfront  russian president vladimir putin has  called the western sanctions on russia  akin to a declaration of war meanwhile  the u.s and the eu are escalating  sanctions including a ban from the u.s  this week on russian oil and natural gas  imports and yet as civilian casualties  in ukraine continue to rise many are  saying that the sanctions  actually aren't even enough where do you  stand on that  i do think that uh sanctions are not  enough in a sense that behavior has not  changed vladimir putin is doubling down  on the war and initially the sanctions  were a deterrent than an instrument to  change behavior  and now the situation is changing in a  sense that putin says i will not stop i  will bomb ukrainian cities i will try to  capture kiev and change the government  in ukraine even though occasionally he  says no it's not my goal  um but  but overall  overall right now sanctions are to uh  increase cost for russian economy for mr  putin and make sure he doesn't have  enough financial resources to continue  the war now mr putin has two fronts  he has unhappy russians at home who are  hit by the sanctions  who would like to protest against the  war because the war is actually quite  unpopular it is not a provoked war it's  a as president biden would say war of  choice  um and the front in ukraine so he needs  a lot of money to pay his policemen in  russia to beat up protesters to pay his  propagandies to convince whoever can be  convinced that this is a just war  and defensive war and then of course to  pay his soldiers in ukraine and in order  to make sure that he doesn't have enough  money indeed u.s has now hit  the most important source of currency  and budget revenues which is oil experts  and u.s is not a big importer of russian  oil but u.s has been joined by the  private sector this is very important to  understand private companies like shell  or bp or myersk  do not have to  stop buying russian oil and transporting  russian oil but they do they they have  actually self-imposed the sanctions on  russian oil which is now becoming toxic  for reputational reasons and in that  sense right now russian oil exports are  disrupted and therefore russia is being  deprived of those resources but there is  now a debate that europe should join  american embargo and that would really  hit uh vladimir putin hard because  europe is the main market for russian  oil and gas the ruble is now worth less  than one u.s penny we've seen long lines  of russians waiting for atm machines  we've seen predictions that russian  inflation will hit up to 17 this year  and people are stocking up on medicines  about 55 percent of which are imported  uh do you believe that the hardships  that are being imposed on ordinary  russians as a result of the sanctions  will be effective uh if anything in  fueling dissent against putin or  is his hold on power  uh so strong that it won't matter  so regimes like this change behavior or  get replaced through two sources a  palace coup by the elite or indeed by  mass protests when the policemen  don't want to shoot  and so mr putin is afraid of both  elites are very unhappy imagine you've  been building a company for 20 or 30  years and it's destroyed in one week  which for example has happened to many  russian businesses now with the  population as you rightly said  population is unhappy as well  but then again mr putin is smart here he  plays divide and rule with the elite and  threatens uh jaylen and poisoning them  but also he invests a lot in  brainwashing the population saying  you're suffering but this is economic  aggression by the west and we are  innocent we are a victim  or he also invests in uh riot police and  so you're unhappy about the fact that  your deposits are frozen and you cannot  afford the medicines and i think that  you point pinpointed the very very very  important issue because for many  russians the main two expanded consumer  expenditures are food and medicines and  medicines are important and whichever  are not imported are also priced to  reflect their global price of medicine  and so in that sense real russians are  now going below the poverty line but can  they protest against that the answer is  no because the putin's police is still  well paid and happy to beat up the  protesters and so unless mr putin is  deprived of fiscal revenue which is  petra dollars in the end of the day  he will double down another piece of  this is the impact of economic sanctions  not just on russia but on the broader  global arrangement of power and global  economic circumstance uh the wall street  journal recently predicted that  sanctions will have quote the biggest  impact on the global economy of any such  moves since  1945 because of the sheer size of course  of the russian economy we're already  seeing fuel prices rise uh in the eu and  in the united states the question though  for many including myself is  is the price of sanctions worth the cost  that they'll have for the rest of the  world  well as they say in russia remember  those happy pandemic times right so this  is much bigger than koveta  this is uh in europe people think about  this as 1939. and indeed already now we  have more than two million refugees from  ukraine that's bigger than the refugee  crisis of 2015 2016. and the expected  number as of today is 5 million this is  the biggest refugee crisis in  europe since 1945 and on top of that mr  putin has nuclear weapons and so in that  sense in that sense it's really a  historic moment  which is somewhat similar in 1939 and so  a lot of countries say whatever the  price of sanctions we need to stop this  aggressor because this aggressor is not  going to be appeased and this indeed  this is true because uh putin invaded  ukraine in 2014  and people thought well he's got what he  wanted that's fine he'll go back and  he'll never  uh invade us again but what has happened  the problem is if your app is an  aggressor like in 1938 you may get  another aggression and yet another  aggression and that's why the west tries  to fight this battle now you're right  that people talk too much about uh the  pain of higher oil and gas prices for  europe but it's going to be a much  bigger pain for developing countries  which don't have such big coffers and  access to that uh markets and on top of  the oil there is also grain because both  russia and ukraine are biggest exporters  of grain and those exports will be  disrupted for sure so things like uh  things like  oil prices grain prices some other  things and of course this massive  refugee flows these are not uh not  outcomes we would love to see  but the alternative is that there'll be  more crisis like this because as i said  mr putin has shown  he doesn't like peace he prefers war to  cooperation trade and investment do you  believe that the international community  is able to offer moscow an exit s